That is a **big pivot**, but surprisingly little of the engineering work is wasted. In some ways, greenfield VMware VCF 9 gives you a cleaner place to apply what we discovered instead of trying to retrofit it into the existing `dev.local` architecture.

The important conceptual change is that I would stop treating the existing environment as the architecture we must transform and start treating it as the **source of requirements and constraints** for the new environment.

Your first-pass VCF 9 model sounds roughly like:

```text
                    Enterprise / Upstream
                    Identity / PKI / DNS
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │      VCF 9          │
                 │                     │
                 │ Management Domain   │
                 │ "Domain Segment"    │
                 │                     │
                 │ vCenter             │
                 │ NSX                 │
                 │ VCF Operations      │
                 │ VCF Automation      │
                 │ Core Infrastructure │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                 controlled dependencies
                            │
              ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
              ▼                           ▼
       Workload Domain(s)          Workload Domain(s)
          Segment 1                     Dev
              │                           │
        protected/core              development
         workloads                   workloads
```

And this changes one of the most important things we've been wrestling with: **the management plane becomes something we can design correctly from Day 0**, rather than figuring out how to bolt MFA, identity, DNS, certificates, reverse proxies, Keycloak, privileged access, and segmentation onto an inherited VMware environment.

A lot of our DAG actually survives. The nodes change, but the questions don't. DNS/TLD, PKI/sub-CA, identity authority, privileged MFA, `sa`/`da` accounts, Keycloak, network trust boundaries, HAProxy, RKE2, Day-0 recovery, service dependencies, Azure identities, OP identities, and disaster recovery are still requirements. What disappears are many of the ugly **migration-induced dependencies** created by `dev.local`, existing vCenters, legacy IdP configuration, and existing workload placement.

There's also a potentially much cleaner architectural separation now:

**Management Domain = infrastructure control plane.** Extremely restricted access, dedicated privileged identities/MFA, VCF/vCenter/NSX management, DNS/NTP/PKI dependencies carefully defined, and as little dependency on workload services as possible.

**Segment 1 workload domain = controlled production/core services.** Keycloak, GitLab, Jira/Confluence, infrastructure applications, potentially RKE2 platform services, etc., subject to whatever security boundary we establish.

**Dev workload domain = developer workloads.** Developers get the flexibility they require without their environment becoming part of the dependency chain required to operate or recover VCF itself.

That also immediately brings our **Day-0/Day-1 bootstrap problem** back to center stage. We should deliberately identify what VCF needs before things such as Keycloak, RKE2, GitLab, AAP, DNS automation, HAProxy, etc. are available. That was already emerging from our dependency analysis, but now we can design it rather than discover it after deployment.

One thing I would *not* do yet is start drawing the final VCF architecture. First I would build a **VCF 9 greenfield foundation DAG**. Something like:

```text
Organization / Security Requirements
                │
                ▼
       Naming / TLD Strategy
                │
        ┌───────┴────────┐
        ▼                ▼
       DNS              PKI
        │                │
        └───────┬────────┘
                ▼
       Network Boundaries
                │
       ┌────────┼─────────┐
       ▼        ▼         ▼
 Management  Segment1    Dev
  Network     Network   Network
       │
       ▼
   VCF 9 Foundation
       │
       ├── Management Domain
       ├── Identity
       ├── Privileged Access/MFA
       ├── vCenter
       ├── NSX
       ├── Operations
       └── Recovery/Backup
                │
                ▼
       Workload Domains
          ┌─────┴─────┐
          ▼           ▼
       Segment1       Dev
          │           │
          ▼           ▼
     Core Services   Dev Services
```

Then we can ask the question that the previous architecture made extremely difficult:

> **If everything is gone and we have racks of powered-off servers, network connectivity, installation media, and credentials, what is the exact dependency order required to rebuild the enterprise?**

That is going to expose whether DNS, PKI, AD, RSA, Keycloak, HAProxy, VCF, RKE2, AAP, GitLab, etc. are sitting at the correct layer.

And I think there's an even bigger opportunity here: **don't copy the old `Domain → Segment1 → Dev` implementation into VCF simply because those are the security zones you have today.** Preserve the *security requirements* of those zones, but let VCF 9/NSX architecture determine how those requirements should actually be implemented in the greenfield design.

So yes—curve ball, but a pretty useful one. The months we've spent untangling identity, MFA, PKI, DNS, segmentation, dependency order, RKE2, HAProxy and recovery have essentially produced the **requirements discovery exercise for the greenfield VCF architecture**.

