## But `tc.org AD` ≠ `VCF Management Domain`

This terminology is going to be **very important when you talk to Security**.

When VMware says:

> **Management Domain**

it does **not** mean:

> Windows/Active Directory domain where administrators live.

A VCF Management Domain is a **VCF infrastructure construct**. The first vSphere cluster of a VCF instance becomes its Management Domain and hosts the core VCF management components. Additional VI workload domains then host workloads. ([VMware Blogs][2])

I would explain it to your security manager this way:

> **The VCF Management Domain is the secure control plane of the private cloud. It isn't our Windows domain. It's where the systems that control the cloud live.**

That's probably the simplest explanation.

Conceptually:

```text
                     tc.org
                Enterprise Identity
                       │
              ┌────────┴────────┐
              │                 │
       On-Prem Users       Azure Users
              │                 │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       │
                Identity / SSO
