Yes. **Now we are at the right place to start engineering the bottom of the DAG.** And after checking the current VCF 9 deployment model, there is one correction to our mental picture that helps considerably:

For a **new VCF 9 deployment**, Broadcom's VCF Installer appliance is the bootstrap mechanism. It replaced Cloud Builder. It can consume a reusable **JSON deployment specification**, validate it, and deploy the initial management cluster and VCF components. A new VCF 9 management cluster requires at least **four hosts**; storage can be vSAN, NFS, or VMFS on FC depending on the supported design. ([VMware Blogs][1])

So I would **not** start by stuffing Ansible + Terraform + DNS + registry + everything else into one giant recovery VM. We should determine what is truly required to get **VCF operational**, and only then determine what the bootstrap appliance needs.

