### Separate three different things

This distinction will prevent us from overengineering the bootstrap VM:

```text
                    VCF 9
                      ↑
               VCF INSTALLER
                      ↑
       ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
       │              │              │
   Installer       Software       Deployment
   prerequisites     depot           config
       ↑              ↑              ↑
       └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                      ↑
              OUR AUTOMATION
                      ↑
               Bootstrap VM
                      ↑
══════════════ DAY-0 BOUNDARY ══════════════
                      ↑
             FUNCTIONING ESXi
```

**VCF Installer prerequisites** are things VMware requires.

**Bootstrap services** are things we have to supply because of our environment—air gap, DNS loss, artifact storage, credentials, etc.

**Our automation** is what makes reconstruction repeatable without an engineer manually clicking through everything.

Those shouldn't get mixed together.

For example, Terraform might ultimately have **nothing to do with installing the first VCF instance**. If VCF Installer already owns that operation, writing Terraform to duplicate it could actually make recovery more complicated.

Ansible might still be extremely useful for:

```text
ESXi survives
      ↑
deploy bootstrap VM
      ↑
configure bootstrap DNS/NTP if necessary
      ↑
bring offline depot online
      ↑
deploy VCF Installer OVA
      ↑
perform prerequisite validation
      ↑
provide/generated deployment JSON
      ↑
invoke Installer
      ↑
VCF 9
```

And then Terraform may become much more important **above VCF**, when we're creating the VM/network resources necessary to satisfy the RKE2 infrastructure requirements.

