## Your lab-install question is the really important one

I would **not** choose between:

**A. manually install VCF and reverse-engineer it**

or

**B. attempt perfect automated IaC deployment on the first try.**

I'd deliberately do both, sequentially.

### Lab Build #1 — Learn VCF

Use the VCF Installer interactively.

Our objective isn't automation yet. It's understanding exactly what VCF asks us for.

```text
Known Lab Infrastructure
          ↑
VCF Installer UI
          ↑
Enter everything manually
          ↑
Validate
          ↑
Correct mistakes
          ↑
Deploy VCF
          ↑
WORKING VCF #1
```

During that installation we document:

* every question the Installer asks;
* every FQDN;
* every IP;
* every VLAN/network;
* every credential class;
* every DNS record;
* every storage selection;
* every appliance-sizing choice;
* every software binary;
* every validation/precheck failure;
* every external dependency we discover.

Most importantly, **download/save the resulting VCF Installer JSON**.

Broadcom specifically designed VCF 9 to support this workflow: the Installer can generate a reusable JSON specification, and VCF Operations documentation describes the JSON as something that can be saved for future builds/reference. ([VMware Blogs][1])

That becomes our first known-good specimen.

### Lab Build #2 — Prove the JSON

Destroy VCF #1.

Don't modify the environment by hand to help ourselves.

Start again at our boundary:

```text
Functional ESXi
       ↑
Recovery VM
       ↑
Bootstrap DNS
       ↑
Offline Depot
       ↑
VCF Installer
       ↑
Known-good JSON
       ↑
VCF #2
```

This time use **Deploy using JSON Spec**.

Broadcom even documents reuse of a downloaded JSON spec when rebuilding/replacing the Installer. One significant warning for our design: the generated JSON can contain credentials in **plain text**, so we absolutely cannot casually treat that file as a normal Git artifact. 

If Build #2 succeeds, we've proven something extremely important:

> **The VCF JSON plus our external dependencies is sufficient to reproduce the VCF state.**

Now we're doing reconstruction rather than documentation.

### Lab Build #3 — Automate the bootstrap

Now we automate everything **around** the Installer.

```text
          recovery/
              │
     ┌────────┼─────────┐
     ▼        ▼         ▼
   config   artifacts  secrets
     │        │
     └────┬───┘
          ▼
     bootstrap
          │
          ├── Validate ESXi
          ├── Validate storage
          ├── Validate network
          ├── Validate Stratum-1 NTP
          │
          ├── Start DNS
          ├── Load DNS records
          │
          ├── Start offline depot
          ├── Validate binaries
          │
          ├── Deploy VCF Installer
          ├── Validate VCF JSON
          │
          └── Start VCF deployment
                    ↑
                 VCF #3
```

VCF Installer exposes APIs for specification validation, resource calculation and starting the VCF installation, so this isn't necessarily UI automation or some brittle Selenium-style process. ([Broadcom Developer][3])

**That's where Ansible may earn its place.**

Not because Ansible installs VCF, but because Ansible could orchestrate the recovery environment that allows the **VCF Installer to install VCF**.

