### I like your "couple dozen times" idea

Because the first four tests aren't really four tests that happen once. They're **maturity stages**.

**Test 1 — Learn.** Manually deploy through VCF Installer. Record every input, prerequisite, mistake, precheck, dependency and correction. Get to a healthy VCF. Capture the known-good deployment JSON.

**Test 2 — Reproduce.** Reset the lab to the boundary. Feed the known-good JSON into VCF Installer. Prove that the configuration reproduces VCF without relying on remembered installation decisions.

**Test 3 — Automate.** Build the recovery VM. Bootstrap DNS, expose the offline software depot, deploy/prepare VCF Installer, validate prerequisites, supply the JSON and reduce human interaction as far as practical.

**Test 4 — Recover.** Destroy everything above the boundary. Pretend the organization just called us after the cyber event. Start with only the protected/offline recovery artifacts and reconstruct VCF.

Then repeat Test 4.

Again.

And again.

That's where the interesting engineering discoveries will happen.

If attempt #7 fails because someone forgot that a particular DNS record had been manually created six weeks earlier, **that's not a failed exercise — we've discovered an undocumented dependency**. Add it to the source of truth/automation and run again.

Eventually the goal becomes almost boring:

```text
